    (("Matthew", 3, 16), ("Genesis", 1, 2), "'the Spirit of God' over the face of the waters — the identical phrase, over water again; offered as a resonance a first reader might hear, not as a claim Matthew makes"),
    (("Matthew", 3, 1), ("Matthew", 2, 1), "paraginetai — the verb that brought the magi to Jerusalem brings the herald into the wilderness, and then the king to the river (3:13)"),
    (("Matthew", 3, 10), ("Matthew", 7, 19), "almost word for word (Matthew adds 'therefore' here): John's test of a tree becomes Jesus' test of a prophet at the end of the Sermon"),
    (("Matthew", 3, 15), ("Matthew", 5, 20), "dikaiosynē — 'all righteousness' in Jesus' first recorded sentence, and the measure the Sermon says must be exceeded"),
    (("Matthew", 3, 3), ("Isaiah", 40, 3), "the punctuation crux: the Hebrew's parallel ('in the wilderness… in the desert') puts the wilderness with the ROAD, the Greek puts it with the VOICE — and Matthew reads 'his paths' where Isaiah and the Greek both read 'our God'"),
    (("Matthew", 3, 17), ("Psalms", 2, 7), "'Jehovah said to me: you are my son, this day I have begotten you' — the coronation psalm behind the first clause of the voice"),
    (("Matthew", 3, 17), ("Isaiah", 42, 1), "'my chosen, in whom my soul delights' — and the clause that follows it is 'I have put my spirit upon him', which is what verse 16 has just described"),
    (("Matthew", 3, 4), ("Malachi", 3, 23), "'Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and terrible day' — Malachi 4:5 in English Bibles, 3:23 in the Hebrew numbering this library follows"),
    (("Matthew", 2, 18), ("Genesis", 37, 35), "ma'en + nacham — the Hebrew of the verse Matthew quotes has Rachel 'REFUSED to be comforted', the same two roots as Jacob refusing comfort over her son Joseph"),
    (("Matthew", 2, 18), ("Genesis", 37, 30), "einennu, 'he is not' — the cry over the empty pit at Dothan, and the singular the Hebrew still has in Jeremiah's line, which Matthew's Greek turns plural"),
    (("Matthew", 2, 18), ("Genesis", 35, 19), "Rachel buried 'on the way to Ephrath, that is, Bethlehem' — why her weeping is heard over the children of this town in particular"),
