    (("Matthew", 2, 23), ("Isaiah", 11, 1), "netser, the 'shoot' from the stump of Jesse — the oldest and strongest proposal for a 'Nazarene' prophecy that exists in no book"),
    (("Matthew", 2, 6), ("2 Samuel", 5, 2), "'you shall shepherd my people Israel' — the clause Matthew's quotation adds to Micah is what the tribes said to DAVID at Hebron"),
    [["2 Thessalonians", 1, 1], ["1 Thessalonians", 1, 1], "the same three senders — Paul, Silvanus and Timothy — writing again to the same congregation months later; the second letter keeps looking back at the first"],
    [["2 Thessalonians", 1, 2], ["1 Thessalonians", 1, 1], "the greeting the first letter cut short — 'grace to you and peace', and it stopped — filled out here to the full formula, 'from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ'; the first of the verbal overlaps behind the authorship question"],
    [["2 Thessalonians", 1, 3], ["1 Thessalonians", 1, 3], "the thanksgiving reopened: faith and love again, but 'we OUGHT to give thanks… as is fitting' where the first letter simply gave thanks"],
    [["2 Thessalonians", 1, 4], ["1 Thessalonians", 1, 6], "endurance under thlipsis — received 'in much affliction with joy' in the first letter, and boasted of to the other congregations of God here"],
    [["2 Thessalonians", 1, 7], ["Zephaniah", 1, 15], "the Lord revealed from heaven 'in flaming fire' meets the Old Testament day of wrath — the verse the Latin turned into the Dies Irae"],
    [["2 Thessalonians", 1, 9], ["1 Thessalonians", 1, 10], "'the wrath that is coming', from which Jesus rescues, named here from the other side as 'eternal destruction, away from the face of the Lord'"],
    [["2 Thessalonians", 1, 11], ["1 Thessalonians", 1, 5], "'work of faith… with power' — the prayer reaching back into the first letter's own thanksgiving, 'not in word only but also in power'"],
    [["1 Peter", 1, 1], ["James", 1, 1], "'the Dispersion' — both General Epistles open by addressing believers as the scattered people of God, exiles and twelve tribes abroad"],
    [["1 Peter", 1, 3], ["2 Corinthians", 1, 3], "'Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ' — the Jewish blessing-formula opening both letters, word for word"],
    [["1 Peter", 1, 7], ["James", 1, 3], "the tested genuineness (dokimion) of faith — tried like gold in the fire here, producing endurance there; the same word for a proving trial"],
    [["1 Peter", 1, 19], ["John", 1, 29], "the lamb without blemish whose blood ransoms — 'as of a lamb', and 'behold, the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world'"],
    [["1 Peter", 1, 23], ["James", 1, 18], "born again through the living WORD — 'through the living and abiding word of God' here, 'he gave us birth by the word of truth' there"],
    [["1 Peter", 1, 24], ["James", 1, 10], "'all flesh is grass… the flower fell' (Isaiah 40) — the fading flower set against the enduring word here, against the rich man there"],
    [["2 Peter", 1, 1], ["1 Peter", 1, 1], "the two letters of Peter open as a pair — 'Simon Peter, slave and apostle' echoing 'Peter, apostle', and the second greeting deliberately recalling the first"],
    [["2 Peter", 1, 1], ["Titus", 1, 1], "'a slave and apostle' — the humble title paired with the high one, the same opening pattern as 'Paul, a slave of God and apostle of Jesus Christ'"],
    [["2 Peter", 1, 10], ["1 Thessalonians", 1, 4], "eklogē, election — 'make your calling and election firm' here, and the readers' 'election' inferred from the gospel's visible power there; the confirming set beside the choosing"],
    [["2 Peter", 1, 16], ["John", 1, 14], "eyewitnesses of his majesty — 'we were eyewitnesses' of the transfigured glory here, 'we beheld his glory, glory as of the only Son' there; the two apostolic claims to have SEEN"],
    [["2 Peter", 1, 17], ["Mark", 1, 11], "'This is my Son, my beloved' — the Father's voice at the Transfiguration, the same word first heard from heaven at the baptism"],
    [["2 Peter", 1, 19], ["Revelation", 2, 28], "the MORNING STAR — the phōsphoros that rises in the heart as the prophetic word's dawn here, and the risen Christ's own promise, 'I will give him the morning star', there"],
    [["James", 1, 2], ["Matthew", 5, 12], "joy in trial — 'count it all joy when you meet trials' beside the Beatitudes' 'rejoice and be glad' when persecuted; James reads like the Sermon on the Mount in another key"],
    [["James", 1, 5], ["Matthew", 7, 7], "'let him ask of God… and it will be given' — all but quoting Jesus, 'ask, and it will be given you'"],
    [["James", 1, 17], ["Matthew", 7, 11], "'every good gift is from above, from the Father' beside 'your Father in heaven gives good things to those who ask him'"],
    [["James", 1, 22], ["Matthew", 7, 24], "the hearer who DOES — 'be doers of the word, not hearers only' beside the wise builder who 'hears these words of mine and does them'"],
    [["James", 1, 12], ["Revelation", 2, 10], "'the crown of life' promised to the one who endures — the same crown held out to the faithful 'unto death'"],
    [["James", 1, 27], ["Isaiah", 1, 17], "pure religion as care for orphans and widows — James's redefinition of worship echoing Isaiah's 'defend the orphan, plead for the widow' against empty sacrifice"],
    [["Hebrews", 1, 2], ["Colossians", 1, 16], "the Son as agent of creation — 'through whom he made the ages' here, 'in him all things were created' there; the same claim two ways"],
    [["Hebrews", 1, 2], ["John", 1, 3], "'through whom' all was made — the Son of Hebrews and the Word of John, each the one through whom everything came to be"],
    [["Hebrews", 1, 3], ["Colossians", 1, 15], "the Son who shows God exactly — 'the exact imprint of his substance' beside 'the image of the invisible God'"],
    [["Hebrews", 1, 3], ["Colossians", 1, 17], "'upholding all things by the word of his power' beside 'in him all things hold together' — the Son who sustains what he made"],
    [["Hebrews", 1, 10], ["Genesis", 1, 1], "'You, Lord, in the beginning laid the foundation of the earth' — a psalm to Yahweh the Creator read to the Son, back into the 'in the beginning' of Genesis"],
    [["Hebrews", 1, 1], ["John", 1, 1], "the two great prologues — God's final word spoken 'in a Son', and 'the Word was God'; each opens on the Son before the world"],
    [["Philemon", 1, 1], ["Colossians", 1, 1], "Timothy the co-sender — Philemon and Colossians were written together and carried the same journey, Onesimus travelling home with both"],
    [["Philemon", 1, 5], ["Colossians", 1, 4], "'your love and the faith you have toward the Lord Jesus and toward all the holy ones' — almost word for word in the twin letter's thanksgiving"],
    [["Philemon", 1, 6], ["Philippians", 1, 5], "koinōnia — the 'fellowship of your faith' here, the 'partnership in the gospel' there; the sharing that becomes action"],
    [["Philemon", 1, 20], ["Philippians", 1, 8], "splanchna — 'refresh my heart in Christ' here, 'the affection of Christ Jesus' there; the inward parts as the seat of love"],
    [["Philemon", 1, 23], ["Colossians", 1, 7], "Epaphras — the Colossian who founded the church, greeting from prison here and named 'our beloved fellow servant' there"],
    [["2 Timothy", 1, 2], ["1 Timothy", 1, 2], "Timothy — the 'beloved child' of the last letter and the 'true child' of the first; affection where the earlier word was standing"],
    [["2 Timothy", 1, 3], ["Romans", 1, 9], "'God whom I serve… unceasingly I remember you in my prayers' — the same near-verbatim thanksgiving, thirty years and a prison apart"],
    [["2 Timothy", 1, 6], ["1 Timothy", 1, 18], "the gift 'through the laying on of my hands', recalling the prophecies spoken over Timothy at his commissioning"],
    [["2 Timothy", 1, 9], ["Titus", 1, 2], "grace and life given 'before times eternal' — the exact phrase shared by the two letters, a gift settled before time began"],
    [["2 Timothy", 1, 10], ["1 Timothy", 1, 1], "'our Saviour Christ Jesus' — the Pastorals' title, and the appearing that 'abolished death and brought life to light'"],
    [["2 Timothy", 1, 13], ["Titus", 1, 9], "'the pattern of SOUND words' held here, the 'sound teaching' guarded there — hygiainō, the Pastorals' medical metaphor"],
    [["Titus", 1, 1], ["Romans", 1, 1], "doulos, slave — 'a slave of God' here, the only place Paul so styles himself, against his usual 'a slave of Christ Jesus'"],
    [["Titus", 1, 3], ["1 Timothy", 1, 1], "'God our Saviour' — sōtēr applied to the Father, the Pastorals' habit, shared word for word with the opening of 1 Timothy"],
    [["Titus", 1, 4], ["1 Timothy", 1, 2], "'my true child' — gnēsios teknon, the same word for Titus 'according to a common faith' and Timothy 'in faith'; Paul's two lieutenants as his genuine sons"],
    [["Titus", 1, 7], ["Philippians", 1, 1], "episkopos, the overseer — named here as one office with the 'elder', and greeted (with the deacons) at Philippi"],
    [["Titus", 1, 9], ["1 Timothy", 1, 10], "the SOUND (healthy) teaching — hygiainō, the Pastorals' shared medical metaphor for right doctrine"],
    [["Titus", 1, 10], ["Galatians", 1, 7], "'those of the circumcision' who unsettle whole households — the same party that troubled the Galatian churches with 'a different gospel'"],
    [["1 Timothy", 1, 1], ["Jude", 1, 25], "'God our Saviour' — sōtēr applied to God the Father, a mark of the Pastorals, and the note Jude ends on: 'to the only God our Saviour'"],
    [["1 Timothy", 1, 2], ["2 John", 1, 3], "'grace, mercy, peace' — the three-part greeting, with 'mercy' added to Paul's usual 'grace and peace'; the same three stand together in the opening of 2 John"],
    [["1 Timothy", 1, 3], ["Ephesians", 1, 1], "Ephesus — where Timothy is left to stop the false teachers, and the city named (though absent from the earliest manuscripts) at the head of the circular letter"],
    [["1 Timothy", 1, 13], ["Galatians", 1, 13], "the persecutor's own account — 'a blasphemer and persecutor and violent man' here, met from outside there: 'I persecuted the church of God violently and tried to destroy it'"],
    [["1 Timothy", 1, 15], ["Luke", 1, 47], "'Christ came into the world to save sinners' answering the Magnificat's 'my spirit rejoices in God my Saviour' — the salvation the child was named for"],
    [["1 Timothy", 1, 17], ["Romans", 1, 23], "aphthartos, the IMMORTAL / incorruptible God — the doxology's 'King of the ages, immortal, invisible' beside the 'glory of the incorruptible God' that idolatry exchanged"],
    [["Matthew", 1, 1], ["Genesis", 2, 4], "'the book of the genesis' — Matthew opens the Gospel with the identical phrase (biblos geneseōs) that the Greek Old Testament uses of creation, 'the book of the genesis of heaven and earth'; the New Testament begins with the word that begins the Old"],
    [["Matthew", 1, 3], ["Genesis", 38, 26], "Tamar — the woman who forced Judah's hand and was declared 'more righteous than I', set by Matthew into the line of the Messiah, the first of his four irregular women"],
    [["Matthew", 1, 5], ["Ruth", 1, 16], "Ruth the Moabite — 'your people shall be my people' — a Gentile grafted into Israel and into David's line, and so into the genealogy of Christ"],
    [["Matthew", 1, 11], ["Jeremiah", 22, 30], "Jechoniah — 'write this man down as childless', said Jeremiah of the king through whom Matthew nonetheless runs the royal line to Jesus; the curse and the line held in tension"],
    [["Matthew", 1, 21], ["Luke", 1, 31], "'you shall call his name Jesus' — the naming command given to Joseph in a dream here, and to Mary by Gabriel there; the two annunciations, from the father's side and the mother's"],
    [["Matthew", 1, 23], ["Luke", 1, 27], "the virgin — Isaiah's parthenos quoted here as fulfilled, and Mary twice called parthenos there; Luke's plain Greek settles what Isaiah's Hebrew almah left open"],
    [["1 John", 1, 1], ["John", 1, 1], "'that which was from the beginning… the word of life' beside 'in the beginning was the Word' — the letter re-sounds the Gospel's prologue, the same author's two openings on the eternal Word"],
    [["1 John", 1, 1], ["Genesis", 1, 1], "'from the beginning' (ap' archēs) reaching back past 'in the beginning' (en archē) — the Word that Genesis presupposes and John names, before the first day"],
    [["1 John", 1, 2], ["John", 1, 4], "'the life was made manifest' beside 'in him was life, and the life was the light of men' — the life that was with the Father, now seen and handled"],
    [["1 John", 1, 3], ["1 Corinthians", 1, 9], "koinōnia — 'fellowship with the Father and with his Son' here, 'called into the fellowship of his Son' there; the sharing that is the aim of the letter"],
    [["1 John", 1, 5], ["John", 1, 5], "light and darkness — 'God is light, and in him is no darkness at all' beside 'the light shines in the darkness, and the darkness did not overcome it'"],
    [["1 John", 1, 7], ["Revelation", 1, 5], "the blood that frees from sin — 'the blood of Jesus his Son cleanses us from all sin' beside 'him who freed us from our sins by his blood'; the two Johannine ends of one image"],
    [["1 Thessalonians", 1, 3], ["Colossians", 1, 4], "faith, love and hope — the triad's first datable appearance here, and again in the Colossian thanksgiving, where hope is the GROUND of the other two rather than a third item beside them"],
    [["1 Thessalonians", 1, 3], ["Colossians", 1, 29], "kopos, labour to exhaustion — the noun here, the verb there of Paul's own work: 'I labour, struggling'"],
    [["1 Thessalonians", 1, 1], ["Philippians", 1, 1], "no apostolic title in either opening — the two letters (with 2 Thessalonians) where Paul's standing is not under attack; this one is barer still, three names and no description at all"],
    [["1 Thessalonians", 1, 1], ["Galatians", 1, 1], "the credential absent here and defended in the first six words there — read in date order, the title appears when it is contested"],
    [["1 Thessalonians", 1, 1], ["2 Corinthians", 1, 19], "Silvanus — Paul's formal Latin name for the Silas of Acts, named in both letters as one of the three who preached in Achaia and Macedonia"],
    [["1 Thessalonians", 1, 5], ["1 Corinthians", 1, 18], "the gospel arriving as POWER rather than as speech — 'not in word only', and 'the word of the cross is the power of God'"],
    [["1 Thessalonians", 1, 10], ["Zephaniah", 1, 15], "'the wrath that is coming' — the same day met from the far side, in the verse the Latin turned into the Dies Irae"],
    [["Colossians", 1, 15], ["Genesis", 1, 26], "eikōn, image — the Septuagint's word for humankind made in God's image; the hymn says that what Adam was made to be, Christ is"],
    [["Colossians", 1, 19], ["Ephesians", 1, 23], "plērōma, fullness — the word the Colossian teachers used for the ranks of beings between God and the world, and which Paul says settled entirely in one man"],
    [["Colossians", 1, 26], ["Ephesians", 1, 9], "the mystery hidden and now made plain — the same structure in both letters, and in both its content concerns the nations"],
    [["Colossians", 1, 24], ["Philippians", 1, 29], "rejoicing in sufferings — written from the same imprisonment; suffering not merely borne but counted a share and a gift"],
    [["Colossians", 1, 6], ["Genesis", 1, 28], "'bearing fruit and growing' — the creation mandate turning up as a description of a message spreading, then applied to the readers themselves at v10"],
    [["Philippians", 1, 5], ["1 Corinthians", 1, 9], "koinōnia — the business-partnership word; in Corinth of sharing in Christ, here nearly literal, since the Philippians had been sending Paul money from the first day"],
    [["Philippians", 1, 18], ["Galatians", 1, 8], "rivals preaching Christ from poisonous motives earn a shrug — 'and in this I rejoice' — where a different GOSPEL earns a double curse; Paul distinguishes the content from the credit"],
    [["Philippians", 1, 1], ["Romans", 1, 1], "'slaves of Christ Jesus' — the same self-description as Romans, but here with no 'apostle' beside it, and shared level with Timothy"],
    [["Philippians", 1, 20], ["Romans", 8, 19], "apokaradokia, the craned neck — Paul's own hope in prison, and creation itself straining for the revealing of God's children; the word's only two occurrences"],
    [["Philippians", 1, 8], ["Acts", 1, 18], "splanchna, the inward parts — the strongest affection-word in Greek, and the same noun used literally of Judas's entrails"],
    [["Ephesians", 1, 14], ["2 Corinthians", 1, 22], "sealed, and given the arrabōn — the signet in wax and the down payment, arriving together in both letters; here the deposit is on an INHERITANCE"],
    [["Ephesians", 1, 3], ["2 Corinthians", 1, 3], "'Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ' — the Jewish berakah opening, used in place of a thanksgiving in both letters"],
    [["Ephesians", 1, 7], ["1 Corinthians", 1, 30], "apolytrōsis, redemption — the ransom paid to free a slave; there one of four things Christ BECAME for us, here what we HAVE in him"],
